t to you know  make everything  one substance and then everything else  just modes of that substance  what i do like about hegel is that  there's a there's an attempt at least to  get  everything in there and not say this  isn't really real this isn't really real  this isn't really real this isn't really  real  but then you know it's precisely that  that  um bringing everything together in one  big thing that i  tend to reject in hegel i like the  dialectical development but not  where it ends up  so this is a very important question in  the philosophy of human nature  and it's asked by emmy star uh what do  you think about the self  are we rational animals what is the  difference between  human beings and animals and  i guess you know you usually  traditionally what we've done  is we've said human being is within  the kingdom of animals but we are  rational beings  so that's our thing that sets us apart  and then you can say well what is what  is it to be rational what is rationality  itself  and i think that for me  uh and this is going to be very you know  sketchy here because i'm  talking about it very quickly when we  also look at the other what we call  propria the  things that are specific to human beings  um  you know like like laughter like  religion like um the forms of social  organization that we have the the  almost infinite plasticity of our  our nature and comparison other animals  i want to say that those must somehow  reflect what rationality consists in so  rationality cannot just be logic  or things along those lines and that  wouldn't fit in very well with you know  how aristotle views  logos or a reason you know it's not just  reason but also speech and it has to do  with our  recognition of values and how we respond  to them  i would also say this i think that  oftentimes you can find a  continuum where animals are doing  particularly what we call the higher  animals are doing  some of the things that human beings do  but not doing them quite at the same  level and i think that not being at the  same level is something very important  you know oftentimes people want to say  well there's got to be a very strict  dividing line here and here  and i think that maybe it's not quite  quite so cut and dry as that  this is another interesting question  that gets posed in terms of the  philosophy of religion  uh and also in some other areas as well  i suppose you could say it's an  epistemological question with some very  practical  applications and it's from version 191  do you think use of psychedelic drugs  like mushrooms or lsd  is a legitimate way to gain religious or  personal  insight and i have to say yes and no  mostly no um i  myself i tend to to view these things as  you know the the workings of uh  i see this in a materialistic way you  know drugs  alter your brain chemistry they they  allow you to  maybe see things that otherwise you  wouldn't you wouldn't be paying  attention to they'll produce  hallucinations  which may have meaning or relevance but  but you're getting out of  the experience what you're actually  bringing into  and projecting on to it so i tend to  think that these things mean a lot more  within cultural contexts rather than  just being you know the consumeristic  thing for  you know uh bored kids who  who want to you know drop some acid and  and you know see some some interesting  colors i think that  they can be very meaningful within uh  strong cultural contexts yeah  but i don't think that anything is going  on there that exceeds the realm of the  the purely material so  i would say you you get out of it what  you put into it if perhaps  you know you were ready to have some  sort of insight  but some things were holding you back  and  uh the use of these and we don't  necessarily just have to think about um  psychedelic drugs you could think about  ones that open people up to be more  empathetic like  like ecstasy does maybe that could  allow a person to do some things but i  think you know it's a dangerous way to  go  and probably not productive in most  cases and certainly not something i  would  be a big advocate of  so this is a very interesting question i  would say  in both the philosophy of religion and  also something important for the history  of philosophy insofar as  revealed religion has played a dynamic  role  in the development of philosophical  systems  and movements and viewpoints from  chandler simmons he says what are the  philosophical reasons for thinking that  god would reveal himself  through written word to his created  creature so that's getting pretty  specific and i'm going to talk about it  in a bit more  general form um what are philosophical  reasons  it's going to depend on what you count  as philosophy but i think that something  that's particularly important to  philosophy  is that philosophy becomes aware  in its practice of its own limitations  and it's got this kind of universalistic  almost totalizing way of saying i want  to take in  everything i want to understand  everything and when philosophy becomes  adequately self-critical it realizes it  might not actually have  all the tools required to do that  and this is one explanation of what  happened you know when judaism  christianity islam  intersect with philosophy that um  philosophy becomes aware in a very acute  way of its own insufficiencies its need  for being informed by something  uh outside of itself but not totally  foreign to itself  you know in some respects a greater  rationality a super irrationality that  that  to appears many to be uh irrationality  and so that that's one you know  line of reasoning that would fill that  out and you you can see that in  you know the early christian fathers who  are approaching philosophy you can see  that also in hegel in his discussion of  revealed religion you can see this in a  lot of people  i think that's one way of going at this  there's others as well but  that's a particularly compelling one for  me
 

